// The boidflock package: flocks of small birds, flying round a home point,
// over ground that rises and falls, and how each flaps its wings. A program
// reaches it with 'import boidflock', makes a 'Flock' of some birds round a
// 'home' point, steps it in fixed steps, and each frame asks each bird for
// its 'BirdPose': where it is, which way it faces, how its wings are beating.
//
// It is Reynolds's boids ("Flocks, Herds, and Schools", SIGGRAPH 1987) on the
// topological neighbourhood Ballerini and others measured in starlings (PNAS
// 2008: each bird follows its nearest 6 or 7 neighbours, not all within a
// distance), with these steering terms, each an acceleration in m/s2, summed
// and limited (flock.cone has the numbers and what each is):
//
//   separation   away from any bird within 1.4 m
//   alignment    towards the neighbours' mean velocity
//   cohesion     towards the neighbours' centre
//   waypoint     towards a point its flock is travelling to, a point within the
//                home's range that changes every 25 to 40 s
//   home         a soft leash: nothing while within 60 per cent of the home
//                radius, then a pull growing as the square of the excess
//   ground       a look ahead along the velocity: if the ground there is
//                within the clearance, a push up and away from rising ground
//   wander       a small drift that turns slowly, so no flock is a conga line
//
// A bird cannot hover: its speed is held between a minimum and a maximum
// (each bird's a little its own). Its wings: a bird is either flapping or
// gliding; it flaps faster when it climbs and slower when it descends, and
// glides (its wings held out, a slow slight beat) when it has speed to spare
// or after a burst of flapping. The flap's phase is integrated here from the
// beat rate, so a renderer draws wings at the phase and amplitude it is given
// and never differentiates.
//
// Conventions: metres, y up, a bird faces along its velocity; the ground's
// height is asked of the caller as a function of (x, z), whatever it is (flat
// ground, a terrain's heightfield with its hill).
//
//   boidflock.cone   this overview
//   flock.cone       Flock, Bird, the steering and the wings

mod boidflock;

import libc;
import collections use *;
import geomath use *;
